<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<metadata>

  <!--
    AbstractTag<T> declares an abstract set(Span, T), which erases to set(Span, Object). Its three
    subclasses override it with the concrete type - StringTag.set(Span, String) and so on - which
    Java resolves through a bridge method and C# cannot express, so each subclass is reported as
    not implementing the abstract member it plainly does implement (CS0534, three times).

    The abstract declaration is removed rather than the subclasses, because the subclasses are the
    useful part: Tags exposes about twenty static fields typed StringTag, IntTag, BooleanTag and
    IntOrStringTag - Tags.HttpUrl, Tags.SpanKind, Tags.Error - and removing them would take the
    whole semantic-tag vocabulary with them. Each subclass keeps its own strongly typed Set, which
    is the overload a caller wants anyway.

    What is lost is calling Set through an AbstractTag or ITag reference, without knowing which
    kind of tag it is. Nothing does that; the tags are used as the constants they are.

    The declaration on the Tag interface goes too. Removing it from AbstractTag alone just moves
    the error - the class then fails to implement the interface member instead of the abstract one
    (CS0535) - because Tag<T> declares the same erased set(Span, Object). Both go, so AbstractTag
    still implements ITag and the hierarchy is unchanged; only the untyped setter is absent.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.opentracing.tag']/interface[@name='Tag']/method[@name='set']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.opentracing.tag']/class[@name='AbstractTag']/method[@name='set']" />

  <!--
    AutoFinishScopeManager and AutoFinishScope are an alternative scope manager whose Activate and
    Active return the concrete AutoFinishScope where the IScopeManager interface returns IScope.
    Java bridges the covariance; C# has no covariant interface implementation, so every member is
    reported as a return-type mismatch (CS0738).

    Both types are marked deprecated in the OpenTracing release itself, and neither is what
    dd-sdk-android uses - AndroidTracer builds on ThreadLocalScopeManager, which binds cleanly and
    is left alone. Removing a deprecated alternative is cheaper than hand-writing the marshalling
    for it.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.opentracing.util']/class[@name='AutoFinishScopeManager']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.opentracing.util']/class[@name='AutoFinishScope']" />

</metadata>
